# Hesperus MQTT ACL
#
# Two identities with deliberately different power:
#
#   hesperus-board     — the STM32 firmware. Full read/write on its own
#                        topics: it must publish status/telemetry AND
#                        subscribe to commands.
#
#   hesperus-dashboard — the browser client (dashboard/hooks/useMQTT.ts).
#                        READ ONLY. Its credentials are compiled into the
#                        public JS bundle and are therefore effectively
#                        public — anyone can read them out of devtools. That
#                        is acceptable ONLY because this ACL forbids it from
#                        publishing: the worst an attacker can do with them
#                        is watch telemetry, not issue board commands.
#                        Without this ACL, handing the browser working
#                        credentials would re-open exactly the hole the
#                        2026-08-18 MQTT auth work closed (anonymous clients
#                        publishing erase_wifi/set_wifi/firmware_update).
#
# The FastAPI server connects as hesperus-board too (it must publish
# commands) — see server/app/mqtt/client.py.

user hesperus-board
topic readwrite device/stm32/#
# 2026-08-24: the FastAPI server also connects as hesperus-board, and it is
# the ONLY publisher of the dashboard/* feeds (routes.py publishes
# dashboard/images/new + dashboard/analysis/new; scheduler/notify.py
# publishes dashboard/schedules/updated). Without a write grant here every
# one of those publishes was SILENTLY DENIED by the broker — mosquitto drops
# an ACL-denied PUBLISH without returning an error to the publisher, and
# paho/fastapi-mqtt therefore logs nothing, so the server looked like it was
# publishing fine. Net effect: the gallery, the analysis panel, the schedule
# list and the power state never updated live; only a manual page refresh
# (REST, unaffected by MQTT ACLs) showed new data.
#
# This is the exact mirror of the 2026-08-19 bug documented in the
# hesperus-dashboard section below: that one denied the browser's SUBSCRIBE,
# this one denied the server's PUBLISH. Fixing the read side alone left the
# feed dead from the other end. When adding a new dashboard/* topic, grant
# BOTH: write here for the server, read below for the browser.
topic write dashboard/#
# $SYS read is required by the container healthcheck, which does
# `mosquitto_sub -t '$SYS/#' -C 1 -W 3` (docker-compose.prod.yml). Without
# this the subscribe is silently denied, no message ever arrives, the 3s
# wait times out, and the container is marked unhealthy forever — which in
# turn blocks `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` and fails deploys.
# $SYS is broker metadata (uptime, client counts), not device data.
topic read $SYS/#

user hesperus-dashboard
topic read device/stm32/#
# 2026-08-19: the dashboard actually subscribes to FOUR "dashboard/*"
# topics (dashboard/app/board/[id]/page.tsx useMQTT() call), not
# device/stm32/# — that only covered raw board telemetry/logs. The gallery
# never updated live because the server's "new image" publish
# (routes.py: mqtt_topic_dashboard_images = "dashboard/images/new") was
# being subscribed to by the browser but silently DENIED by this ACL — no
# error surfaces client-side, mosquitto just drops the SUBSCRIBE, so it
# looked like a frontend bug for hours before anyone thought to check here.
# Board logs worked the whole time because device/stm32/# already covered
# those, which is exactly what made this look like an isolated gallery bug
# rather than an ACL gap affecting every dashboard/* feed.
topic read dashboard/images/new
topic read dashboard/analysis/new
topic read dashboard/logs
topic read dashboard/schedules/updated
